      "title": {
        "en": "Utility Types: Partial",
        "id": "Utility Types: Partial"
      },
      "description": {
        "en": "Use Partial to make all properties of an interface optional.",
        "id": "Gunakan Partial untuk membuat semua properti interface menjadi opsional."
      },
      "instructions": {
        "en": "# Utility Types: Partial\n\n## Concept\nTypeScript provides \"Utility Types\" to transform existing types. `Partial<T>` takes a type `T` and creates a new type where all properties are optional (`?`).\n\n| Utility | Description |\n|---|---|\n| `Partial<T>` | Makes everything optional |\n| `Required<T>` | Makes everything required |\n| `Readonly<T>` | Makes everything unchangeable |\n\n## Code Example\n```typescript\ninterface User { name: string; age: number; }\n\n// Now name and age are optional (?)\ntype DraftUser = Partial<User>;\n```\n\n## Real World Scenario\nIn an \"Update Profile\" feature (PATCH request), you rarely update *every* field. You might just send `{ email: \"new@test.com\" }`. `Partial<User>` is the perfect type for this, as it allows any subset of the `User` fields.\n\n## Your Task\n1. Define interface `FullReport` with properties `title`, `passed`, and `failed` (all are required properties).\n2. Create a type alias `DraftReport` that uses `Partial<FullReport>`.\n3. Create an object `myDraft` of type `DraftReport` composed of **only** `{ passed: 5 }`.\n4. Assign `myDraft.passed` to the `result` variable.\n\n\n> **Playground note:** This exercise checks the runtime `result`; it does not perform full TypeScript semantic validation. Review the type syntax here, and use `tsc --noEmit` in a real project.",
        "id": "# Utility Types: Partial\n\n## Concept\nTypeScript menyediakan \"Utility Types\" untuk mengubah tipe yang sudah ada. `Partial<T>` mengambil tipe `T` dan membuat tipe baru di mana semua properti menjadi opsional (`?`).\n\n| Utility | Deskripsi |\n|---|---|\n| `Partial<T>` | Membuat semuanya opsional |\n| `Required<T>` | Membuat semuanya wajib |\n| `Readonly<T>` | Membuat semuanya tidak bisa diubah |\n\n## Code Example\n```typescript\ninterface User { name: string; age: number; }\n\n// Sekarang name dan age bersifat opsional (?)\ntype DraftUser = Partial<User>;\n```\n\n## Real World Scenario\nDalam fitur \"Update Profile\" (request PATCH), kamu jarang memperbarui *setiap* field. Kamu mungkin hanya mengirim `{ email: \"new@test.com\" }`. `Partial<User>` adalah tipe yang sempurna untuk ini, karena mengizinkan subset apa pun dari field `User`.\n\n## Tugas Kamu\n1. Definisikan interface `FullReport` dengan properti `title`, `passed`, dan `failed` (semua wajib).\n2. Buat type alias `DraftReport` yang menggunakan `Partial<FullReport>`.\n3. Buat objek `myDraft` bertipe `DraftReport` yang **hanya** berisi `{ passed: 5 }`.\n4. Simpan `myDraft.passed` ke dalam variabel `result`.\n\n\n> **Catatan playground:** Latihan ini memeriksa `result` saat runtime, bukan melakukan semantic validation TypeScript secara lengkap. Review type syntax-nya, lalu gunakan `tsc --noEmit` di project sungguhan."
      },
      "hints": {
        "en": [
          "Easily create variations of existing types, such as making every property in a large interface optional at once.",
          "Use the `Partial<T>` built-in utility type to transform a strict interface into a flexible \"draft\" shape.",
          "Define `type DraftReport = Partial<FullReport>` to create an object that only needs a subset of the original properties."
        ],
        "id": [
          "Buat variasi dari tipe yang sudah ada dengan mudah, seperti membuat setiap properti dalam interface yang besar menjadi opsional sekaligus.",
          "Gunakan utility type bawaan `Partial<T>` untuk mengubah interface yang ketat menjadi bentuk \"draft\" yang fleksibel.",
          "Definisikan `type DraftReport = Partial<FullReport>` untuk membuat objek yang hanya membutuhkan sebagian dari properti aslinya."
        ]
      },
      "htmlContent": "<div class=\"patch-tool\">\n  <h3>Data Patcher</h3>\n  <div id=\"payload\">Ready to patch...</div>\n</div>",
      "starterCode": "// 1. Define interface FullReport { title: string, passed: number, failed: number }\n// Your code here\n\n// 2. Define DraftReport = Partial<FullReport>\n// Your code here\n\n// 3. Create myDraft: DraftReport = { passed: 5 }\n// Your code here\n\n// 4. result = myDraft.passed\n// Your code here",
      "testCases": [
        {
          "description": "Returns the property from a Partial object",
          "expectedOutput": "5",
          "isHidden": false
        }
      ],
      "solution": "interface FullReport {\n  title: string;\n  passed: number;\n  failed: number;\n}\ntype DraftReport = Partial<FullReport>;\nconst myDraft: DraftReport = { passed: 5 };\nconst result = myDraft.passed ?? 0;",
      "tags": ["typescript", "utility-types", "partial"]
    },
    {
      "slug": "ts-fundamentals-boss",
      "type": "TYPESCRIPT",
      "difficulty": "HARD",
      "category": "ts-fundamentals",
      "xpReward": 60,
      "order": 2309,
      "title": {
        "en": "Scenario: Build Typed Test Users",
        "id": "Skenario: Buat Typed Test User"
      },
      "description": {
        "en": "Build a typed user factory while keeping compile-time structure separate from runtime validation.",
        "id": "Buat typed user factory sambil membedakan compile-time structure dari runtime validation."
      },
      "instructions": {
        "en": "# Scenario: Build Typed Test Users\n\n## Why this matters\nA factory can keep controlled test users consistent. Its TypeScript contract documents supported roles, while the implementation owns deterministic defaults and formatting.\n\nIt does **not** prove that an email is deliverable, that external data matches the interface, or that the product accepted the user. Those require runtime validation and product assertions.\n\n## Your task\n1. Define `interface User` with `id: number`, `email: string`, and `role: \"admin\" | \"guest\"`.\n2. Create `createUser(id: number, role?: \"admin\" | \"guest\"): User`.\n3. Default a missing role to `\"guest\"` with `??`.\n4. Format each controlled email as `` `user_${id}@test.com` ``.\n5. Create `const adminUser` with ID `5` and role `\"admin\"`.\n6. Create `const guestUser` with ID `6` and no role argument so the default path runs.\n7. Declare `const result` with the exact string `user_5@test.com | guest`.\n\n> **Playground note:** This exercise verifies the requested TypeScript syntax and runtime result. It does not perform full semantic type checking. Use `tsc --noEmit` in a real project.",
        "id": "# Skenario: Buat Typed Test User\n\n## Kenapa ini penting\nFactory bisa menjaga controlled test user tetap konsisten. TypeScript contract-nya mendokumentasikan role yang didukung, sementara implementation menangani default dan formatting yang deterministik.\n\nContract tersebut **tidak** membuktikan email bisa menerima pesan, external data sesuai dengan interface, atau produk menerima user tersebut. Semua itu tetap membutuhkan runtime validation dan product assertion.\n\n## Tugas kamu\n1. Definisikan `interface User` dengan `id: number`, `email: string`, dan `role: \"admin\" | \"guest\"`.\n2. Buat `createUser(id: number, role?: \"admin\" | \"guest\"): User`.\n3. Beri default role `\"guest\"` dengan `??` saat role tidak tersedia.\n4. Format setiap controlled email sebagai `` `user_${id}@test.com` ``.\n5. Buat `const adminUser` dengan ID `5` dan role `\"admin\"`.\n6. Buat `const guestUser` dengan ID `6` tanpa argument role supaya default path dijalankan.\n7. Declare `const result` dengan string persis `user_5@test.com | guest`.\n\n> **Catatan playground:** Latihan ini memeriksa syntax TypeScript yang diminta dan runtime result. Latihan ini tidak melakukan semantic type checking secara lengkap. Gunakan `tsc --noEmit` di project sungguhan."
      },
      "hints": {
        "en": [
